{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Measure by measure.</b> Most of the commentators say it is from the root of סאה, and the meaning is measure, and it is a doubled word like ירקרק (Leviticus 13:49), מטלטלך (Isaiah 22:17):" "The heh of <b>תריבנה</b> is over Samaria, and behold afterward it is a fortified city:" "<b>When she sent them forth.</b> Some say that it is she corrupted herself with her weapons of war, like ובעד השלח יפלו (Joel 2:8). But the correct view is that it is from the root of שלחיך (Song of Songs 4:13), and the witness is 'in the day of the east wind,' and behold it is so: by the measure she measured for herself, for she made strife even with the people of her villages, and they are 'those she sent forth,' therefore HaShem thrust her away, like אשד הוגה מן המסילה (II Samuel 20:13). And behold the heh stands in place of the yod of the root, like ידה הדה (Isaiah 11:8):" "<b>In the day of the east wind.</b> An east wind:"], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "This comment is textually opaque; rendered hyper-literally. The logic of 'the heh' and its syntactic connection to the following clause is unclear in the source."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:55:27.572116532Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}